<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 1</a> &gt; Level 7</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 1 Level 7 Walkthrough: The Game Night Agency</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a recorded playthrough of the current build, 489 seconds of video at 1080p. Two floors, five puzzle stations and one safe. Every timestamp below is the video's own clock, not the in-game timer. The in-game clock starts at 0:00 about 17 seconds into the recording.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={1} current={7} variant="jump" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>The safe code in The Game Night Agency is <b>55793</b>. It is written on a crumpled note lying on the bottom shelf of the board-game shelf, next to the Scrabble box. A small white object that is very easy to walk past.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Solve the checkerboard packing puzzle on the low table → note <b>H=C</b> (0:41).</li>
        <li>Clear the 4×4 card board (16 cards) → the carved wooden box opens (0:46).</li>
        <li>Build the fox in the tangram box → a <b>Plate</b> marked <b>M=T</b> (2:03).</li>
        <li>Finish the briefcase block puzzle → the <b>CAN</b> board game and a photograph (2:45).</li>
        <li>Pick up the small white object on the bottom shelf of the game shelf (7:13), smooth it out, read <b>55793</b>, then type it on the keypad beside the door.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/mPYL_dJS2V4" title="Rooms & Exits Chapter 1 Level 7 The Game Night Agency walkthrough" allowfullscreen loading="lazy"></iframe>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-hero-agency.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 1 Level 7 The Game Night Agency: the two-level cafe interior with board-game shelves, tables and the neon sign" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Game Night Agency is one room pretending to be two. It is a cafe-cum-office for a company that runs game nights, and the game drops you on the lower floor between a row of tables and a long shelf of board games. Stairs on the right lead up to the office half: two desks, monitors, a glass cabinet, a cork board. There is no corridor and no second room. Every clue you need is inside these two floors.</p>
    <p>What makes Level 7 different from the four rooms before it is that nothing chains in a straight line. Several puzzle stations are open from the first minute, each one hands you an object, and the objects do not point at each other. The one number that matters. The safe code. Is not on any of the puzzles. It is a crumpled scrap lying on the bottom shelf of the game shelf, and our recording only notices it at 7:13, when everything else is already done.</p>
    <p>So the honest way to play it is not "follow the steps". It is: clear every station on the lower floor first, because that is where the drawer, the safe and the exit door all are.</p>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:18</span>
      <strong>Stand still and read the room before you touch anything</strong>
      You start on the lower floor with five open stations around you: the low table with the checkerboard, the cabinet with the card board, the shelf of board games, the plant shelf, and the low unit on the left wall. The stairs to the office half are on the right. Nothing here is hidden behind a door, which is why players waste their first two minutes opening the obvious objects and finding nothing.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-hero-agency.jpg" alt="The two-level Game Night Agency interior at the start of Chapter 1 Level 7" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=18s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:18</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:22</span>
      <strong>The checkerboard on the low table is a packing puzzle</strong>
      A recessed square board with half a checkerboard already in place, and loose checkerboard-patterned pieces sitting around it. Drag each piece into the board until the pattern closes. There is no chess move to find. The loose pieces are the whole puzzle, and the board rejects them if they overlap. Clear it and a note lifts out of the board: <b>H=C</b> at 0:41.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-checkerboard.jpg" alt="The checkerboard packing puzzle with loose black and white pieces" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=22s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:22</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:46</span>
      <strong>The 4×4 card board: sixteen cards, flipped two at a time</strong>
      Next to the carved wooden box is a board of sixteen face-down cards in a four-by-four grid. Turn two, keep the pair, put them back if they do not match. It is the one station in the level with no code attached and nothing to collect. What it gates is the carved box on the same cabinet. In our recording the box opens only after the board is cleared, and the tangram inside is the next puzzle.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-card-pairs.jpg" alt="The sixteen-card memory board in The Game Night Agency" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=46s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:33</span>
      <strong>The fox tangram in the carved box</strong>
      A wooden frame of triangles and quadrilaterals in three wood tones, and a fox's head as the target image. Drag the loose pieces from around the frame into it until the fox is complete: ears, muzzle, eyes. It is the cleanest puzzle in the level. No timer, no penalty, and the pieces do not rotate away from where they belong.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-fox-tangram.jpg" alt="The fox-head tangram puzzle inside the carved wooden box" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=93s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 2:03</span>
      <strong>Out comes the Plate. And it carries the second note</strong>
      Solving the tangram releases a metal <b>Plate</b>, and the plate is engraved with the second letter pair: <b>M=T</b>. This is the pattern of the whole room. You are not collecting keys, you are collecting substitutions, and each one arrives attached to an object rather than as a loose piece of paper.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-plate.jpg" alt="The metal Plate marked M=T released by the fox tangram" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=123s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:03</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 2:09</span>
      <strong>The briefcase block puzzle: coloured blocks and a D-pad</strong>
      Open the briefcase on the table and you get a grid with coloured blocks pre-placed and a ring of blocks waiting on the left. You move blocks with the D-pad on the right, not by dragging, and there is a reset arrow above it if you corner yourself. Fit every block into the grid and the briefcase gives up its contents at 2:45: the <b>CAN</b> board game, then a photograph a second later.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-block-panel.jpg" alt="The coloured block grid puzzle inside the briefcase" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=129s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:09</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 2:54</span>
      <strong>The plant shelf, and the drawer under it</strong>
      Three potted plants on a brick shelf lit by two hanging lamps, with drawers underneath. This is the station people photograph and then forget: the <b>Picture</b> you picked up at 2:47 belongs here, and the shelf is where the third note turns up at 3:27. Search the drawers while you are standing here. One of them is the closest thing the lower floor has to a stash.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-plant-shelf.jpg" alt="The plant shelf with drawers in The Game Night Agency" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=174s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:54</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 3:35</span>
      <strong>The board-game shelf is a joke with a purpose</strong>
      Five boxes on a purple shelf, all of them real games with one letter bent: a Bingo box, a Scrabble box, a Quiz box, a Twister box and a Monopoly box spelled <em>Monoboly</em>. The shelf is the room's theme statement rather than a lock. Look at it, take the <b>CAN</b> box's slot in your inventory as read, and move on. The puzzle work on this floor is on the walls, not the merchandise.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-board-games.jpg" alt="The shelf of spoof board games including a box spelled Monoboly" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=215s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:35</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 4:00</span>
      <strong>The green light grid on the back wall</strong>
      A grid of dark squares on the back wall that lights up green as you work it. It reads as decoration on the first pass and it is not: it is a full station, and it takes the longest of any single puzzle in the room. Our recording is still on it at 4:59. Work the squares systematically from one corner; there is nothing timed here and the back arrow undoes a misstep.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-green-grid.jpg" alt="The green light grid puzzle on the back wall" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=240s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 5:12</span>
      <strong>The small screen with the toggle and the segmented letters</strong>
      A framed screen on the lower floor: a white toggle switch on the left, green segmented letterforms on the right, three pale circles underneath. This is the one station our recording uses without fully explaining. The player toggles it, watches the letters settle, and leaves. Nothing enters the inventory here. Treat it as a look-and-note stop: copy whatever letters it shows before you walk away.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-switch-panel.jpg" alt="The toggle-and-letters panel in The Game Night Agency" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=312s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 5:50</span>
      <strong>The sliding-block puzzle that unlocks the drawer</strong>
      A three-by-three well with a blue U-shaped block, a red U-shaped block and a single black square, and a D-pad with a red button below it. Shuffle the blocks with the D-pad until the board settles into its solved state. The shape is the classic sliding-block problem, but on a board small enough to brute-force. In our recording the puzzle is finished at 6:12 and the drawer below it opens at 6:17.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-slide-blocks.jpg" alt="The sliding-block puzzle with blue and red U-shaped blocks" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=350s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5:50</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 6:17</span>
      <strong>Open the drawer for the gold bars. The code is not in here</strong>
      The drawer in the low grey cabinet pulls out with two gold bars inside and sparkle markers on it. Take them and move on. This is the level's trap: the drawer looks like the payoff and it is not. The safe code is a separate scrap of paper sitting on the bottom shelf of the game shelf, and if you stop at the drawer you will never find it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-drawer.jpg" alt="The open drawer with two gold bars in The Game Night Agency" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=377s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:17</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 6:25</span>
      <strong>The fuse box with eight switches</strong>
      A grey panel of horizontal switches, some up and some down. Set them so the panel reads as solved. Our recording settles it in about fifteen seconds, which means the correct configuration is close to what you find. Nothing drops into the inventory; the panel is part of the room's power-up chain, and the lights come back on around the counter.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-fuse-box.jpg" alt="The eight-switch fuse box in The Game Night Agency" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=385s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:25</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 6:30</span>
      <strong>The Game Night sign: only some letters are glowing</strong>
      Over the counter, a blue <em>Game</em> above a pink <em>Night</em>, both inside two neon rings. Look at it and the game puts the nudge on screen itself: <em>"Only some letters are glowing... Interesting."</em> That is the note puzzle's home. You are meant to read the glowing letters against the six substitutions in your inventory. Do not expect the sign to give you the safe code. It does not.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-neon-sign.jpg" alt="The Game Night neon sign with only some letters glowing" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=390s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 7:00</span>
      <strong>Inventory check: six notes, one photograph, a plate and two board games</strong>
      By 7:00 the inventory is the level's scoreboard. Six letter-pair notes, the plant photograph, the Plate, the CAN box, the two gold bars, and the note from the checkerboard. If you are missing a note, you have missed a station. The six do not come from six obvious places, and two of them arrive attached to objects rather than as paper.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-notes.jpg" alt="The Game Night Agency inventory with six letter-pair notes" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=420s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 16 · 7:37</span>
      <strong>Picked up at 7:13, straightened out at 7:37: 5-5-7-9-3</strong>
      The crumpled paper sits next to the Scrabble box on the bottom shelf of the game shelf. Smooth it out and the digits are written in purple ink, one dash between each: <b>5-5-7-9-3</b>. Five digits, and that is exactly how many the keypad takes. This is the only place in The Game Night Agency where the code appears. It is not repeated on a wall, a board or the neon sign.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-code-paper.jpg" alt="The crumpled note reading 5-5-7-9-3" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mPYL_dJS2V4&t=457s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">7:37</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 17 · 7:50</span>
      <strong>The keypad by the door: 55793</strong>
      The wall safe sits beside the exit door on the lower floor, and its keypad is a plain 0–9 grid with an X and a green tick. Type <b>55793</b> and confirm. The safe swings open and there is a key inside. This is the object the level has been withholding, not another puzzle piece.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/l7-keypad.jpg" alt="The five-digit keypad beside the exit door with 55793 entered" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
