    <h2>The six notes, copied out<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Six notes turn up across the level, each carrying one letter pair. They look like a cipher and players spend a long time trying to turn them into the safe code. They are not the safe code. The set, as it appears in the inventory:</p>
    <div class="cipher">
      {notes.map(([l, n]) => <span>{l}<i>{n}</i></span>)}
    </div>
    <p>What they belong to is the neon sign over the counter on the lower floor: a blue <em>Game</em> over a pink <em>Night</em>, circled in neon, with the game's own nudge appearing on screen when you look at it. <em>"Only some letters are glowing... Interesting."</em> Our recording collects all six notes before it bothers with the sign, and the inventory order is the order the room gives them to you, not an order you type anywhere.</p>

    <h2>Why the code is not on the notes<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>This is the single most common way people lose ten minutes in The Game Night Agency: they treat the six letter pairs as a keypad cipher and try to read H=C as a number. The keypad has no letters on it. It is a plain 0–9 pad with an X and a green tick, five digits wide, and the only five-digit string in the level is on the crumpled note from the game shelf.</p>
    <p>The notes are a different puzzle with a different answer. The safe is the exit; the sign is the flavour. If you have been staring at a letter substitution table trying to make a five-digit code out of it, stop. The paper you want is on the bottom shelf of the game shelf at 7:37, and it reads <b>5-5-7-9-3</b>.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The checkerboard is not chess.</b> The pieces are loose and they get dragged into the recessed board. Players who assume it is a chess position sit there looking for a legal move that does not exist.</li>
      <li><b>The card board is a gate, not a lock.</b> Sixteen cards in a 4×4 grid, flipped two at a time. Nothing in your inventory changes when you clear it. What changes is that the carved wooden box on the same cabinet opens, and the tangram inside is the next thing you need.</li>
      <li><b>The drawer is behind a puzzle you may have skipped.</b> The sliding-block puzzle (blue and red U-shaped blocks, D-pad on the right) is easy to walk past because it sits low on the left wall. No sliding-block solve, no drawer, no two gold bars.</li>
      <li><b>The code is not in the drawer, and it is not on the notes.</b> It is a small white scrap on the bottom shelf of the board-game shelf, next to the Scrabble box. The least dramatic object in the room, and the one that ends the level.</li>
      <li><b>The green grid looks like decoration.</b> A grid of dark squares on the back wall that lights up green as you work them. It is a real station, not a wall pattern.</li>
      <li><b>The door is a decoy.</b> The level does not end by walking through it. It ends when the wall safe next to it opens and the outro plays.</li>
      <li><b>Five digits, not four.</b> Every other room in Chapter 1 uses four-digit codes. This one does not, and the pad will sit there looking broken while you wait for a fourth digit to do something.</li>
    </ul>

